A||N||14||Jonathan||Polyurethane ?||||1||
Z||000000||Jonathan||11-15-2000||12:09 PM||||Doing my homework before I purchase a complete polyurethane bushing kit.  What steps can I take to avoid bushing squeaks?  I know that the grease eventually wears out, and I don't think that the graphite impregnated ones are available for Fieros, (are they)?  Anyone have any tips?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||11-15-2000||01:54 PM||||i have been in the process of rebuilding mine.  i used a teflon grease by permatex.  <P>i also discussed this with Ed Parks, here is what he had to say:<P>"Brian, we chose QuickSilver Anti-Corrosion Grease (Mercury Marine brand)<BR>over a generic or other types. It is part # 92-850735A. Have tried teflon<BR>and silicon with poor results. Can't tell where squeaks come from, but<BR>have had zerk fittings used with mixed results. <P>Sometimes the poly makes it more difficult to align, but shouldn't stop a<BR>good shop. Type of grease won't make a difference, but install could."<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/alien.gif">   <BR>We are not alone!||||reg||1
Z||000002||zeusrekning||11-15-2000||04:09 PM||||will aluminum bushings squeek?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Flash Box||11-16-2000||12:30 PM||||Not to slam polyurtheane bushings or anything but here is my experiecnce with them. First off I had these on my '84 Camaro so maybe different applications will differ. I purchased Energy Suspension and they were good for about the first year. Then the squeaks, snaps, etc. started. I have had to replace the tranny mount and front sway bar bushings because they tore apart. (I don't rip on the car either) my control arm bushings are starting to get small tears in them as well. So this spring I am going back to rubber. Again I am not tring to discourage you I was just posting my experince with them.<P>------------------<BR>"5 out of 4 people have a problem with fractions."||||reg||7
Z||000004||theogre||11-16-2000||08:46 PM||||From a comming cave article....<P>Keep something in mind. allot of people who compair polly are compairing the handling to the old worn bushings they replaced. If you compair to new OE type bushings a large chunk of polly's perported gain is null. (If you account that many polly upgrades also includ ball joint replacement..... the gain is even less.)<P>Even if the polly doesn't last all that long in the sway bar I'd still use it. Sway bars aren't hard to fool with if the bushings wear out. Adding polly to sways is like having a little stiffer bar. Doesn't seem to matter what I put in the sway bar they only last so long. These bushings take a hell of allot of abuse. (Rubber ones may not physically look bad but the performance of them drops off.) <P>Polly in the control arms has to be weighed out real carefully. There will be maintenance issues. just a matter of when they will start. Same for most other non OE choices. (Forget to grease a set of needles and they won't last very long at all.) <P>One place I recomend polly, or even solid, bushings on all 84-87 fiero is the cradel. These bushings don't rotate so they should never squeak. They will make a huge diferance in handling since the cradel won't be moving around anymore. (My own polly cradel set was installed sans any grease at all. no squeeks even on the cold mornings.)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jonathan||11-17-2000||08:42 AM||||When I spoke to Ed Parks on the matter a while back, he told me that for himself, he preferred the same thing.  Poly on the cradle and regular rubber everywhere else.  Has anyone here ever tested a poly setup alongside with a refreshed rubber setup?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fierotag2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-17-2000||09:00 AM||||I've never been able to do head to head kind of testing. I can tell you fresh OE bushings are allot tighter than most people would think.<P>main thing with OE rubber is it has to be installed right. If you mess up this one critical step the bushings won't last.<P>you put the bushing in, insert the center bolt and run the nut up close but don't tighten it yet.<P>next put the car's weight on the suspension. It doesn't matter what method you use as long as the car is fully onto the suspension. Now tighten the center bolts in the A arm bushings.<P>doing this eliminates preload in the bushing that will destroy them in fairly short order. A preloaded bushing will very often tear loose from the inner sleeve in just a few months if not sooner. Once this happens the buching is ruined and handling will likely fall off fairly soon.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||11-17-2000||09:43 AM||||For what it's worth...<P>I just put the el cheapo polyurethane end link bushings in my GT a few months ago.  Just the end link bushings, not the ones that attach the stabilizer bar to the frame.  Low risk, low cost, easily reversible if I don't like it.  I like the results - noticeably better handling, and no squeaks.<P>Next step will be to rebush the stab.-attach brackets.  Not looking forward to this one, because of horror stories about getting the bolts out.  Will probably purchase both the  polyurethane <B>and</B> OE bushings - fairly easy to swap if squeaks become a problem.<P>Won't rebush control arms unless and until they wear out - and very likely will go with OE rubber then.  If I decide to try poly, I'll likely use them in some spare control arms.  <P>Point is - if you can, suggest you try mods  one at a time, see if the results are acceptable / noticeable, and go from there.      <P>Ogre, based on what you have said, you have pretty much sold me on the polyurethane cradle bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||11-17-2000||11:02 AM||||Stu,<P>You shouldn't worry at all about the bolts holding the bar on.  They are easily accessable.  In other words you can get a 6' cheater bar if you need to.  I just removed mine last week, didn't even use a 1/2" drive or the BFH either.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000009||lowCG||11-17-2000||11:17 AM||||I tend to agree with the Ogre,for the most part,and the only place I found there to be a large difference was in the rear control arm bushings.<BR>I really wish that someone would make a set of UHMW bushings,as these wear way better than polyurethane,and don't bind either.The material costs only $20 for a set.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||11-17-2000||11:28 AM||||A project I've got going right now is to make bushings from XL UHMW (XL = Cross Linked). It's supposed to have NVH characteristics better than regular UHMW, while still wearing well and not binding like polyurethane. The downside is that XL is expensive ($85 for a piece 2"x2"x48"). The drawings used to make the XL bushings could just as easily be use to make UHMW bushings though.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rodmcneill||11-17-2000||11:48 AM||||I was reading a while back that Chevrolet had at one time, stiffer bushings avail. for the Citation front end which is basicaly what the Fiero has on the rear. It was the X11 model I believe which was their sporty model.These bushings were supposed to be between poly. and reg. bushings as far as stiffness with less maintenance than the poly. I don`t know if these are still available.||||reg||1
Z||000012||ThatRickGuy||11-17-2000||02:21 PM||||Well, I just ordered my poly befor reading this post, ahh well, anything will be better then what I have now. I was hearing some odd noises while breaking and a little grabbing feel, so I guessed that one of the pads was breaking up and tore into the rotor pretty good. but when I pulled it apart, everything appeared fine. bewildered and agrivated, I smacked the top of the rotor with my hand and it made a noise. giving it a few good wiggles I could see the top a-arm wiggling about 1/8-1/4" the bushings are completely dead. So I hoped online and ordered a set of poly bushings from fierostore, I remebered hearing people complain about squeeking, but hey, if they can make it about 8 months, I'll be happy. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||mshill||11-17-2000||07:28 PM||||This comes up quite often.  I had to replace the a-arm bushings on my 86SE last year.  Mine don't squeak and I have about 6K miles on them.  I ended up putting poly all around and on the cradle and swaybar.<P>I have no complaints.||||reg||1
Z||000014||MrPBody||11-17-2000||07:32 PM||||On my list of *Things To Do When I Have Time And Money* is to install a set of IRM's high-durometer bushings.  They may be a useful compromise between stock and polyurethane.<BR>||||reg||1
